  ($v$mayco$v$, $v$stoneware-gloss$v$, $v$SW-505$v$, $v$Purple Gloss$v$, $v$mayco-purple-gloss$v$, $v$glaze$v$, $v$Purple Gloss is a highly-pigmented glaze$v$, $v$#2a234b$v$, $v$purple$v$, array[$v$purple$v$]::text[], $v$gloss$v$, $v$opaque$v$, 5, 10, true, $v$Cone 6: Purple Gloss is a highly-pigmented glaze. The stable qualities of this glaze make it ideal for design work and full opaque coverage at mid-range temperatures. Cone 10: Color changes to blue.$v$, $v$https://www.maycocolors.com/product/sw-505-purple-gloss/$v$, null, null, null, $v$https://www.maycocolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/09/sw-505.jpg$v$, $v$Mayco$v$),
  ($v$mayco$v$, $v$stoneware-gloss$v$, $v$SW-506$v$, $v$Bright Blue Gloss$v$, $v$mayco-bright-blue-gloss$v$, $v$glaze$v$, $v$Bright Blue Gloss is a highly-pigmented sky blue gloss glaze$v$, $v$#518fa4$v$, $v$blue$v$, array[$v$blue$v$]::text[], $v$gloss$v$, $v$opaque$v$, 5, 10, true, $v$Cone 6 oxidation (larger image): Bright Blue Gloss is a highly-pigmented sky blue gloss glaze. The stable qualities of this glaze make it ideal for design work and full opaque coverage at mid-range temperatures. Cone 10 reduction (smaller image): Color darkens.$v$, $v$https://www.maycocolors.com/product/sw-506-bright-blue-gloss/$v$, null, null, null, $v$https://www.maycocolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/09/sw-506.jpg$v$, $v$Mayco$v$),
  ($v$mayco$v$, $v$stoneware-gloss$v$, $v$SW-507$v$, $v$Bright Green Gloss$v$, $v$mayco-bright-green-gloss$v$, $v$glaze$v$, $v$Bright Green Gloss is a highly-pigmented chartreuse gloss glaze$v$, $v$#b5b446$v$, $v$green$v$, array[$v$green$v$]::text[], $v$gloss$v$, $v$opaque$v$, 5, 10, true, $v$Cone 6 oxidation (larger image): Bright Green Gloss is a highly-pigmented chartreuse gloss glaze. The stable qualities of this glaze make it ideal for design work and full opaque coverage at mid-range temperatures. Cone 10 reduction (smaller image): Color lightens; changes to light blue in heavy reduction.$v$, $v$https://www.maycocolors.com/product/sw-507-bright-green-gloss/$v$, null, null, null, $v$https://www.maycocolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/09/sw-507.jpg$v$, $v$Mayco$v$),
  ($v$mayco$v$, $v$stoneware-gloss$v$, $v$SW-508$v$, $v$Black Gloss$v$, $v$mayco-black-gloss$v$, $v$glaze$v$, $v$Black Gloss is a highly-pigmented true black gloss glaze$v$, $v$#0d0d0d$v$, $v$black$v$, array[$v$black$v$]::text[], $v$satin$v$, $v$opaque$v$, 5, 10, true, $v$Cone 6 oxidation (larger image): Black Gloss is a highly-pigmented true black gloss glaze. The stable qualities of this glaze make it ideal for design work and full opaque coverage at mid-range temperatures. Glaze turns to satin gloss at cone 5 and high gloss at cone 6. Cone 10 reduction (smaller image): Exhibits some blue hues.$v$, $v$https://www.maycocolors.com/product/sw-508-black-gloss/$v$, null, null, null, $v$https://www.maycocolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/09/sw-508.jpg$v$, $v$Mayco$v$),
  ($v$mayco$v$, $v$stoneware-gloss$v$, $v$SW-509$v$, $v$Dark Green Gloss$v$, $v$mayco-dark-green-gloss$v$, $v$glaze$v$, $v$Dark Green Gloss is a highly-pigmented emerald green gloss glaze$v$, $v$#296139$v$, $v$green$v$, array[$v$green$v$]::text[], $v$gloss$v$, $v$opaque$v$, 5, 10, true, $v$Cone 6: Dark Green Gloss is a highly-pigmented emerald green gloss glaze. The stable qualities of this glaze make it ideal for design work and full opaque coverage at mid-range temperatures. Cone 10: No change.$v$, $v$https://www.maycocolors.com/product/sw-509-dark-green-gloss/$v$, null, null, null, $v$https://www.maycocolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/09/sw-509.jpg$v$, $v$Mayco$v$),
  ($v$mayco$v$, $v$stoneware-gloss$v$, $v$SW-510$v$, $v$Blue Gloss$v$, $v$mayco-blue-gloss$v$, $v$glaze$v$, $v$Blue Gloss is a highly-pigmented royal blue gloss glaze$v$, $v$#294794$v$, $v$blue$v$, array[$v$blue$v$]::text[], $v$gloss$v$, $v$opaque$v$, 5, 10, true, $v$Cone 6: Blue Gloss is a highly-pigmented royal blue gloss glaze. The stable qualities of this glaze make it ideal for design work and full opaque coverage at mid-range temperatures. Cone 10: No change.$v$, $v$https://www.maycocolors.com/product/sw-510-blue-gloss/$v$, null, null, null, $v$https://www.maycocolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/09/sw-510.jpg$v$, $v$Mayco$v$),
  ($v$mayco$v$, $v$stoneware-gloss$v$, $v$SW-511$v$, $v$Pink Gloss$v$, $v$mayco-pink-gloss$v$, $v$glaze$v$, $v$Pink Gloss is a glossy, opaque, medium pink glaze that is highly pigmented and formulated for stable design work at midrange temperatures an$v$, $v$#f48f87$v$, $v$pink$v$, array[$v$pink$v$]::text[], $v$gloss$v$, $v$opaque$v$, 5, 10, true, $v$Cone 6 oxidation (large photo): Pink Gloss is a glossy, opaque, medium pink glaze that is highly pigmented and formulated for stable design work at midrange temperatures and above. Cone 10 reduction (small photo): Glaze breaks over texture. Glaze remains stable.$v$, $v$https://www.maycocolors.com/product/sw-511-pink-gloss/$v$, $v$This glaze is stable, even with heavy application. Can be mixed with other Stoneware Gloss glazes (SW-500 series) to produce additional stable and opaque colors. Layer design work with other Stoneware Gloss series colors to create crisp images that won’t blur or require a clear glaze.$v$, null, null, $v$https://www.maycocolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/03/sw-511.jpg$v$, $v$Mayco$v$),
  ($v$mayco$v$, $v$stoneware-gloss$v$, $v$SW-512$v$, $v$Coral Gloss$v$, $v$mayco-coral-gloss$v$, $v$glaze$v$, $v$Coral Gloss is a glossy, opaque, vibrant coral glaze that is highly pigmented and formulated for stable design work at midrange temperatures$v$, $v$#f74d47$v$, $v$pink$v$, array[$v$pink$v$]::text[], $v$gloss$v$, $v$opaque$v$, 5, 10, true, $v$Cone 6 oxidation (large photo): Coral Gloss is a glossy, opaque, vibrant coral glaze that is highly pigmented and formulated for stable design work at midrange temperatures and above. Cone 10 reduction (small photo): No change. Glaze remains stable.$v$, $v$https://www.maycocolors.com/product/sw-512-coral-gloss/$v$, $v$This glaze is stable, even with heavy application. Can be mixed with other Stoneware Gloss glazes (SW-500 series) to produce additional stable and opaque colors. Layer design work with other Stoneware Gloss series colors to create crisp images that won’t blur or require a clear glaze.$v$, null, null, $v$https://www.maycocolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/03/sw-512.jpg$v$, $v$Mayco$v$),
  ($v$mayco$v$, $v$stoneware-gloss$v$, $v$SW-513$v$, $v$Brown Gloss$v$, $v$mayco-brown-gloss$v$, $v$glaze$v$, $v$Brown Gloss is a glossy, opaque, rich brown glaze that is highly pigmented and formulated for stable design work at midrange temperatures an$v$, $v$#51280c$v$, $v$brown$v$, array[$v$brown$v$]::text[], $v$gloss$v$, $v$opaque$v$, 5, 10, true, $v$Cone 6 oxidation (large photo): Brown Gloss is a glossy, opaque, rich brown glaze that is highly pigmented and formulated for stable design work at midrange temperatures and above. Cone 10 reduction (small photo): Color shifts to olive-brown. The glaze remains stable.$v$, $v$https://www.maycocolors.com/product/sw-513-brown-gloss/$v$, $v$This glaze is stable, even with heavy application. Can be mixed with other Stoneware Gloss glazes (SW-500 series) to produce additional stable and opaque colors. Layer design work with other Stoneware Gloss series colors to create crisp images that won’t blur or require a clear glaze.$v$, null, null, $v$https://www.maycocolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/03/sw-513.jpg$v$, $v$Mayco$v$),
  ($v$mayco$v$, $v$flux$v$, $v$SW-407$v$, $v$Clear Flux$v$, $v$mayco-clear-flux$v$, $v$flux$v$, $v$Clear Flux is a translucent, mobility enhancer that is intended to be layered with glazes$v$, null, $v$clear$v$, array[$v$clear$v$]::text[], null, $v$translucent$v$, 5, 10, true, $v$Cone 6: Clear Flux is a translucent, mobility enhancer that is intended to be layered with glazes. Cone 10: More movement.$v$, $v$https://www.maycocolors.com/product/sw407-clear-flux/$v$, $v$Clear Flux is the most fluid of the Flux products. Take care in applying on vertical surfaces. Mobility will increase along with the number of coats. Avoid application on the bottom third of your vertical ware. Can be used as a stand-alone glaze. Flux is a flowing glaze developed for use with other stoneware glazes to enhance the movement, promote reactions and interesting effects. Apply 2-3 coats of Flux under or over another mid-range glaze to bring about “hidden colors”. Flux can be brushed in layers, glaze trailed, plopped with a brush, and blotted with a sponge. The thicker the application, the more mobility there will be. Additional information$v$, 2, 3, $v$https://www.maycocolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/SW407-Clear-Flux.jpg$v$, $v$Mayco$v$),
  ($v$mayco$v$, $v$stoneware-classic$v$, $v$SW-463$v$, $v$Turquoise Moon Rocks$v$, $v$mayco-turquoise-moon-rocks$v$, $v$glaze$v$, $v$Turquoise dimensional, puffed texture in varying sizes$v$, $v$#85bcb7$v$, $v$turquoise$v$, array[$v$turquoise$v$]::text[], null, null, 5, 10, false, $v$Cone 6 oxidation (larger image): Turquoise dimensional, puffed texture in varying sizes. Cone 10 reduction: Puffed texture may increase. Color darkens. Tips for Use: Layer Together: Moon Rocks can be layered with other Moon Rock colors to create stunning visual effects. Allow the first layer to set slightly (loses its glossiness) before adding the next. Alternate directions of coats (apply one layer vertically and the next layer horizontally). 1-2 coats of each color are recommended, but additional coats will produce a more pronounced puffed pattern. Use with Underglazes and Engobes: Moon Rocks remains dimensional when applied over Underglazes and Stoneware Engobes, preserving its raised texture. For best results, it is recommended to bisque fire the Underglaze or Engobe to cone 04 before applying the Melt Gloop, as this helps with adhesion and ensures the glaze performs effectively during the final firing. Layer with Stoneware Glazes: When used either under or over a glaze, such as Stoneware glazes, the texture may flatten during firing. Despite this change, the technique can still produce interesting and colorful effects. Firing Range: Moon Rocks have a broad firing range from cone 5 to cone 10 (2167°F-2345°F /1186°C-1285°C) in both oxidation and reduction firings. Firing at higher temperatures may result in the glaze texture to increase and colors to darken. Because results can vary based on firing conditions and layering techniques, it’s recommended to test the glaze beforehand. Dinnerware: Moon Rocks are not suitable for dinnerware due to surface texture. However, it can be used on the outside of functional ware, below the lip line.$v$, $v$https://www.maycocolors.com/product/sw463-turquoise-moon-rocks/$v$, null, null, null, $v$https://www.maycocolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/sw463.jpg$v$, $v$Mayco$v$),
  ($v$mayco$v$, $v$stoneware-classic$v$, $v$SW-149$v$, $v$Crackle White$v$, $v$mayco-crackle-white$v$, $v$glaze$v$, $v$Crackle White is a bright white gloss glaze that develops a fine crackle pattern$v$, $v$#bfbebb$v$, $v$white$v$, array[$v$white$v$]::text[], $v$gloss$v$, null, 5, 10, false, $v$Cone 6 oxidation (larger image): Crackle White is a bright white gloss glaze that develops a fine crackle pattern. Works well on stoneware and porcelain bodies and produces crackle patterns at midrange temperatures. Use India ink to highlight or accentuate crackle patterns. Cone 10 reduction (smaller image): Turns an orange-brown and pools white in texture when thick. The glaze does not crackle.$v$, $v$https://www.maycocolors.com/product/sw-149-crackle-white/$v$, null, null, null, $v$https://www.maycocolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/09/sw-149.jpg$v$, $v$Mayco$v$),
  ($v$mayco$v$, $v$stoneware-classic$v$, $v$SW-003$v$, $v$Stoneware Crackle Matte Clear$v$, $v$mayco-stoneware-crackle-matte-clear$v$, $v$glaze$v$, $v$Crackle Matte Clear is a soft matte, semi-transparent glaze that develops a fine crackle pattern$v$, null, $v$clear$v$, array[$v$clear$v$]::text[], $v$matte$v$, $v$transparent$v$, 5, 10, false, $v$Cone 6 oxidation (larger image): Crackle Matte Clear is a soft matte, semi-transparent glaze that develops a fine crackle pattern. Cone 10 reduction (smaller image): No Change.$v$, $v$https://www.maycocolors.com/product/sw-003-stoneware-crackle-matte-clear/$v$, $v$A minimum of two coats are needed to develop a crackle pattern. To highlight the crackle pattern, after firing, wipe black acrylic or ink over the ware and wipe off, allowing the ink to fill the cracks.$v$, null, null, $v$https://www.maycocolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/09/sw-003.jpg$v$, $v$Mayco$v$),
  ($v$mayco$v$, $v$stoneware-classic$v$, $v$SW-403$v$, $v$White Mudcrack$v$, $v$mayco-white-mudcrack$v$, $v$glaze$v$, $v$White Mudcrack is a high-texture glaze that forms a raised, cracked surface$v$, $v$#b5b3b3$v$, $v$white$v$, array[$v$white$v$]::text[], null, null, 5, 10, false, $v$Cone 6 oxidation (larger image): White Mudcrack is a high-texture glaze that forms a raised, cracked surface. The thicker the application, the more textured the surface will be. Cone 10 reduction (smaller image): Texture flattens, color remains white. $ 16.25 QTY/Case: White Mudcrack quantity Add to cart$v$, $v$https://www.maycocolors.com/product/sw-403-white-mudcrack/$v$, $v$Mudcrack was developed to create a highly-texture raised and cracked surface. The thicker the application, the more textured the surface. Mudcrack can be directly applied to bisqueware or in combination with other mid-range glazes. For shown results, apply directly to bisqueware. Heavy application may cause unfired glaze to release from bisqueware. If glaze is thick in the jar, vigorous stirring and shaking will increase fluidity. The amount of texture and cracking that is produced with these glazes is directly a result of how thick the glaze is applied. A thinner application will result in a smoother surface with very fine cracks. A thicker application will result in larger cracks and more tactile texture. Chips shown are fired flat on a white clay body fired to cone 6 oxidation and cone 10 reduction. The choice of clay body, the thickness of glaze application, the firing process, and temperature will affect the fired results.$v$, null, null, $v$https://www.maycocolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/09/sw-403.jpg$v$, $v$Mayco$v$),
  ($v$mayco$v$, $v$stoneware-classic$v$, $v$SW-404$v$, $v$Black Mudcrack$v$, $v$mayco-black-mudcrack$v$, $v$glaze$v$, $v$Black Mudcrack is a high-texture glaze that forms a raised, cracked surface$v$, $v$#2f2e2c$v$, $v$black$v$, array[$v$black$v$]::text[], null, null, 5, 10, false, $v$Cone 6 oxidation (larger image): Black Mudcrack is a high-texture glaze that forms a raised, cracked surface. The thicker the application, the more textured the surface will be. Cone 10 reduction (smaller image): Texture flattens, color remains black. $ 16.25 QTY/Case: Black Mudcrack quantity Add to cart$v$, $v$https://www.maycocolors.com/product/sw-404-black-mudcrack/$v$, $v$Mudcrack was developed to create a highly-texture raised and cracked surface. The thicker the application, the more textured the surface. Mudcrack can be directly applied to bisqueware or in combination with other mid-range glazes. For shown results, apply directly to bisqueware. Heavy application may cause unfired glaze to release from bisqueware. If glaze is thick in the jar, vigorous stirring and shaking will increase fluidity. The amount of texture and cracking that is produced with these glazes is directly a result of how thick the glaze is applied. A thinner application will result in a smoother surface with very fine cracks. A thicker application will result in larger cracks and more tactile texture. Chips shown are fired flat on a white clay body fired to cone 6 oxidation and cone 10 reduction. The choice of clay body, the thickness of glaze application, the firing process, and temperature will affect the fired results.$v$, null, null, $v$https://www.maycocolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/09/sw-404.jpg$v$, $v$Mayco$v$),
  ($v$mayco$v$, $v$stoneware-classic$v$, $v$SW-405$v$, $v$Light Magma$v$, $v$mayco-light-magma$v$, $v$glaze$v$, $v$Light Magma is a sand-colored, high-texture glaze that creates a rough, cratered surface$v$, $v$#a6a092$v$, null, '{}'::text[], null, null, 5, 10, false, $v$Cone 6 oxidation (larger image): Light Magma is a sand-colored, high-texture glaze that creates a rough, cratered surface. The thicker the application, the more textured the surface will be. Cone 10 reduction (smaller image): Texture slightly flattens. Color changes to a cream/gray.$v$, $v$https://www.maycocolors.com/product/sw-405-light-magma/$v$, null, null, null, $v$https://www.maycocolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/09/sw-405.jpg$v$, $v$Mayco$v$),
  ($v$mayco$v$, $v$stoneware-classic$v$, $v$SW-406$v$, $v$Dark Magma$v$, $v$mayco-dark-magma$v$, $v$glaze$v$, $v$Dark Magma is a charcoal-colored, high-texture glaze that creates a rough, cratered surface$v$, $v$#3a3634$v$, null, '{}'::text[], null, null, 5, 10, false, $v$Cone 6 oxidation (larger image): Dark Magma is a charcoal-colored, high-texture glaze that creates a rough, cratered surface. The thicker the application, the more textured the surface will be. Cone 10 reduction (smaller image): Texture slightly flattens. No change in color.$v$, $v$https://www.maycocolors.com/product/sw-406-dark-magma/$v$, null, null, null, $v$https://www.maycocolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/09/sw-406.jpg$v$, $v$Mayco$v$),
  ($v$mayco$v$, $v$stoneware-classic$v$, $v$SW-461$v$, $v$Orange Moon Rocks$v$, $v$mayco-orange-moon-rocks$v$, $v$glaze$v$, $v$Orange dimensional, puffed texture in varying sizes$v$, $v$#f2a280$v$, $v$orange$v$, array[$v$orange$v$]::text[], null, null, 5, 10, false, $v$Cone 6 oxidation (larger image): Orange dimensional, puffed texture in varying sizes. Cone 10 reduction: Puffed texture may increase. Color darkens. Tips for Use: Layer Together: Moon Rocks can be layered with other Moon Rock colors to create stunning visual effects. Allow the first layer to set slightly (loses its glossiness) before adding the next. Alternate directions of coats (apply one layer vertically and the next layer horizontally). 1-2 coats of each color are recommended, but additional coats will produce a more pronounced puffed pattern. Use with Underglazes and Engobes: Moon Rocks remains dimensional when applied over Underglazes and Stoneware Engobes, preserving its raised texture. For best results, it is recommended to bisque fire the Underglaze or Engobe to cone 04 before applying the Melt Gloop, as this helps with adhesion and ensures the glaze performs effectively during the final firing. Layer with Stoneware Glazes: When used either under or over a glaze, such as Stoneware glazes, the texture may flatten during firing. Despite this change, the technique can still produce interesting and colorful effects. Firing Range: Moon Rocks have a broad firing range from cone 5 to cone 10 (2167°F-2345°F /1186°C-1285°C) in both oxidation and reduction firings. Firing at higher temperatures may result in the glaze texture to increase and colors to darken. Because results can vary based on firing conditions and layering techniques, it’s recommended to test the glaze beforehand. Dinnerware: Moon Rocks are not suitable for dinnerware due to surface texture. However, it can be used on the outside of functional ware, below the lip line.$v$, $v$https://www.maycocolors.com/product/sw461-orange-moon-rocks/$v$, null, null, null, $v$https://www.maycocolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/sw461.jpg$v$, $v$Mayco$v$),
  ($v$mayco$v$, $v$stoneware-classic$v$, $v$SW-462$v$, $v$Poppy Moon Rocks$v$, $v$mayco-poppy-moon-rocks$v$, $v$glaze$v$, $v$Poppy dimensional, puffed texture in varying sizes$v$, $v$#ea8a89$v$, null, '{}'::text[], null, null, 5, 10, false, $v$Cone 6 oxidation (larger image): Poppy dimensional, puffed texture in varying sizes. Cone 10 reduction: Puffed texture may increase. Color darkens. Tips for Use: Layer Together: Moon Rocks can be layered with other Moon Rock colors to create stunning visual effects. Allow the first layer to set slightly (loses its glossiness) before adding the next. Alternate directions of coats (apply one layer vertically and the next layer horizontally). 1-2 coats of each color are recommended, but additional coats will produce a more pronounced puffed pattern. Use with Underglazes and Engobes: Moon Rocks remains dimensional when applied over Underglazes and Stoneware Engobes, preserving its raised texture. For best results, it is recommended to bisque fire the Underglaze or Engobe to cone 04 before applying the Melt Gloop, as this helps with adhesion and ensures the glaze performs effectively during the final firing. Layer with Stoneware Glazes: When used either under or over a glaze, such as Stoneware glazes, the texture may flatten during firing. Despite this change, the technique can still produce interesting and colorful effects. Firing Range: Moon Rocks have a broad firing range from cone 5 to cone 10 (2167°F-2345°F /1186°C-1285°C) in both oxidation and reduction firings. Firing at higher temperatures may result in the glaze texture to increase and colors to darken. Because results can vary based on firing conditions and layering techniques, it’s recommended to test the glaze beforehand. Dinnerware: Moon Rocks are not suitable for dinnerware due to surface texture. However, it can be used on the outside of functional ware, below the lip line.$v$, $v$https://www.maycocolors.com/product/sw462-poppy-moon-rocks/$v$, null, null, null, $v$https://www.maycocolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/sw462.jpg$v$, $v$Mayco$v$),
  ($v$mayco$v$, $v$flux$v$, $v$SW-408$v$, $v$White Flux$v$, $v$mayco-white-flux$v$, $v$flux$v$, $v$White Flux is a translucent white, mobility enhancer that is intended to be layered with glazes$v$, null, $v$white$v$, array[$v$white$v$]::text[], null, $v$translucent$v$, 5, 10, true, $v$Cone 6: White Flux is a translucent white, mobility enhancer that is intended to be layered with glazes. Cone 10: More movement.$v$, $v$https://www.maycocolors.com/product/sw408-white-flux/$v$, $v$Take care in applying on vertical surfaces. Mobility will increase along with the number of coats. Avoid application on the bottom third of your vertical ware. Can be used as a stand-alone glaze. Flux is a flowing glaze developed for use with other stoneware glazes to enhance the movement, promote reactions and interesting effects. Apply 2-3 coats of Flux under or over another mid-range glaze to bring about “hidden colors”. Flux can be brushed in layers, glaze trailed, plopped with a brush, and blotted with a sponge. The thicker the application, the more mobility there will be. Additional information$v$, 2, 3, $v$https://www.maycocolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/SW408-White-Flux.jpg$v$, $v$Mayco$v$),
  ($v$mayco$v$, $v$stoneware-classic$v$, $v$SW-460$v$, $v$White Moon Rocks$v$, $v$mayco-white-moon-rocks$v$, $v$glaze$v$, $v$White dimensional, puffed texture in varying sizes$v$, $v$#eae4de$v$, $v$white$v$, array[$v$white$v$]::text[], null, null, 5, 10, false, $v$Cone 6 oxidation (larger image): White dimensional, puffed texture in varying sizes. Cone 10 reduction: Puffed texture may increase. Color darkens. Tips for Use: Layer Together: Moon Rocks can be layered with other Moon Rock colors to create stunning visual effects. Allow the first layer to set slightly (loses its glossiness) before adding the next. Alternate directions of coats (apply one layer vertically and the next layer horizontally). 1-2 coats of each color are recommended, but additional coats will produce a more pronounced puffed pattern. Use with Underglazes and Engobes: Moon Rocks remains dimensional when applied over Underglazes and Stoneware Engobes, preserving its raised texture. For best results, it is recommended to bisque fire the Underglaze or Engobe to cone 04 before applying the Melt Gloop, as this helps with adhesion and ensures the glaze performs effectively during the final firing. Layer with Stoneware Glazes: When used either under or over a glaze, such as Stoneware glazes, the texture may flatten during firing. Despite this change, the technique can still produce interesting and colorful effects. Firing Range: Moon Rocks have a broad firing range from cone 5 to cone 10 (2167°F-2345°F /1186°C-1285°C) in both oxidation and reduction firings. Firing at higher temperatures may result in the glaze texture to increase and colors to darken. Because results can vary based on firing conditions and layering techniques, it’s recommended to test the glaze beforehand. Dinnerware: Moon Rocks are not suitable for dinnerware due to surface texture. However, it can be used on the outside of functional ware, below the lip line.$v$, $v$https://www.maycocolors.com/product/sw460-white-moon-rocks/$v$, null, null, null, $v$https://www.maycocolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/sw460.jpg$v$, $v$Mayco$v$),
  ($v$mayco$v$, $v$flux$v$, $v$SW-401$v$, $v$Light Flux$v$, $v$mayco-light-flux$v$, $v$flux$v$, $v$Light Flux is a translucent cream color when used alone$v$, null, $v$cream$v$, array[$v$cream$v$]::text[], null, $v$translucent$v$, 5, 10, true, $v$Cone 6: Light Flux is a translucent cream color when used alone. Cone 10: The increase in temperature will cause more movement.$v$, $v$https://www.maycocolors.com/product/sw-401-light-flux/$v$, $v$Chip shown over SW-119 Cinnabar. Flux is a flowing glaze developed for use with other stoneware glazes to enhance the movement, promote reactions and interesting effects. Apply 2-3 coats of Flux under or over another mid-range glaze to bring about “hidden colors”. Flux can be brushed in layers, glaze trailed, plopped with a brush, and blotted with a sponge. The thicker the application, the more mobility there will be. Additional information$v$, 2, 3, $v$https://www.maycocolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/09/sw-401.jpg$v$, $v$Mayco$v$),
  ($v$mayco$v$, $v$flux$v$, $v$SW-402$v$, $v$Dark Flux$v$, $v$mayco-dark-flux$v$, $v$flux$v$, $v$Dark Flux is a translucent brown color when used alone$v$, null, $v$brown$v$, array[$v$brown$v$]::text[], null, $v$translucent$v$, 5, 10, true, $v$Cone 6: Dark Flux is a translucent brown color when used alone.  Cone 10: The increase in temperature will cause more movement.  TIP: Chip shown over SW-119 Cinnabar.$v$, $v$https://www.maycocolors.com/product/sw-402-dark-flux/$v$, $v$Flux is a flowing glaze developed for use with other stoneware glazes to enhance the movement, promote reactions and interesting effects. Apply 2-3 coats of Flux under or over another mid-range glaze to bring about “hidden colors”. Flux can be brushed in layers, glaze trailed, plopped with a brush, and blotted with a sponge. The thicker the application, the more mobility there will be. Additional information$v$, 2, 3, $v$https://www.maycocolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/09/sw-402.jpg$v$, $v$Mayco$v$)
) as v(brand, series, code, name, slug, glaze_type, base_color, swatch, family, tags, finish, opacity, cmin, cmax,
       food, notes, url, app, coats_min, coats_max, image, credit)
join public.brands b on b.slug = v.brand
left join public.glaze_series s on s.brand_id = b.id and s.slug = v.series
on conflict (slug) do update set
  brand_id = excluded.brand_id, series_id = excluded.series_id, product_code = excluded.product_code,
  name = excluded.name, glaze_type = excluded.glaze_type, base_color = excluded.base_color,
  swatch_hex = coalesce(excluded.swatch_hex, public.glazes.swatch_hex), color_family = excluded.color_family,
  color_tags = excluded.color_tags, finish = excluded.finish, opacity = excluded.opacity,
  cone_min = excluded.cone_min, cone_max = excluded.cone_max,
  manufacturer_food_safe_claim = excluded.manufacturer_food_safe_claim,
  manufacturer_notes = excluded.manufacturer_notes, official_url = excluded.official_url,
  application_notes = excluded.application_notes, coats_min = excluded.coats_min, coats_max = excluded.coats_max,
  image_url = excluded.image_url, image_credit = excluded.image_credit;

delete from public.glaze_clay_results r using public.glazes g, public.brands b
where r.glaze_id = g.id and g.brand_id = b.id and b.slug = 'mayco' and r.verification_status = 'manufacturer_documented';

